Title: THE VOICE OF ONE
Credit: Written by Dale Joseph
Author: Dale Joseph
Source: from the Voice of One canon
Draft date: 2026-08-18
Contact: The Emergence Institute

====

INT. CHURCH BASEMENT - NIGHT

Chairs stacked against the wall. Lights off.

DELPHINE stands at the basement door, phone in hand. She plays a voice note once, low.

VOICE NOTE (V.O.)
Not tonight.

A man's voice. Unhurried. No name, no number.

Delphine locks the phone. Looks at the stacked chairs.

A CONGREGANT comes down the steps, coat on, expecting a room.

CONGREGANT
What happened.

DELPHINE
We canceled. Maintenance.

CONGREGANT
At eight at night.

DELPHINE
At eight at night.

She shows him the phone — unknown number, a voice note.

CONGREGANT
What did it say.

DELPHINE
Not tonight.

ONE comes down the steps behind them. He does not reach for the phone.

CONGREGANT
So where's the backup.

DELPHINE
Two smaller rooms. Known hosts. No
address in writing. Same checklist.

CONGREGANT
Who tells the rest.

DELPHINE
I do. Quietly. You tell the early ones
the same.

The congregant goes back up the steps.

Delphine turns off the last light. One holds the door.

DELPHINE (CONT'D)
Helen will post the protocol. We follow
it.

She locks the door behind them.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

HELEN at the reference desk after close. One lamp. Her phone face-up.

She types the protocol in plain text, two fingers, no shorthand.

INSERT — PHONE (plain text, white background):
Witness Circle Update:
If a meeting is disrupted, we do not argue online.
We reroute. We document. We continue.

THE RESPONSE PROTOCOL
(for rooms targeted by fear)

1. Cancel immediately if any threat appears.
   Safety is not cowardice.
2. Do not post the threat publicly.
   Do not feed fear attention.
3. Notify attendees through private channels.
   Keep messages factual. No speculation.
4. Reroute to two smaller circles within 48 hours.
5. Document the event quietly.
   Date, time, method. Store it offline.
6. Return to routine next week.
   The goal is normalcy.

BACK TO SCENE

She reads it once. Posts it. Sets the phone down.

INT. FACULTY LOUNGE - NIGHT

A TEACHER sets six chairs in a circle. No sign on the door.

INT. BARBERSHOP - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

A BARBER drags a chair across the floor. Eight people file in after close.

INT. CHURCH KITCHEN - NIGHT

DELPHINE ladles soup. Ten people. Same calm. Same checklist.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Helen's phone lights — a flat list. Times, rooms, names of hosts. Nothing else.

She reads it. Closes the notebook. Switches off the lamp.

INT. ROW HOUSE - DAY

ROSA (40s) opens the door to a MAN in a blazer with a clipboard and a badge.

ONE sits at the kitchen table, papers in front of him, a pencil. He does not get up.

INSPECTOR
District. Routine compliance
follow-up. Unapproved gatherings.
Residential.

ROSA
It's a reading group.

INSPECTOR
That's what the review will
determine.

He slides a form across the table. It wants everything — employer, school, household members, social accounts.

ROSA
For your files.

He leaves. The form sits on the table.

A CHILD watches from the kitchen, eating toast.

Rosa folds the form in half. Does not sign it.

One watches the door. Says nothing.

LATER — a letter slides under the door: RENT REVIEW — LEASE HOLD PENDING.

Rosa reads it standing. Puts it with the form.

LATER STILL — two at the door. A SOCIAL WORKER with a clipboard, a uniformed officer behind her.

SOCIAL WORKER
We received a concern. Unsafe
environment for a minor.

ROSA
What concern.

SOCIAL WORKER
Anonymous report.

Rosa doesn't move from the door.

ROSA
Put it in writing. Case number.
Supervisor's name.

The social worker hesitates.

SOCIAL WORKER
We'll follow up.

They go. Rosa closes the door.

The child is at the table now, toast gone.

ROSA (CONT'D)
Homework.

The child goes.

Rosa straightens the papers — form, letter, everything in order.

One hasn't moved from the table. He watches her work.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - DAY

HELEN reads a printout — an official announcement, syrup-smooth.

INSERT — FLYER:
THE COMMUNITY STABILITY PROGRAM
Support for Families Impacted by Online Harassment.
- mental wellness resources
- community safety liaisons
- rapid response support
- trusted information partners

BACK TO SCENE

Helen circles four lines with a pencil:

Who runs it.
What they collect.
What happens if you refuse.
Who benefits.

She forwards the flyer to the circle with two words.

INSERT — PHONE (plain text):
It's a trap.

INT. COMMUNITY CENTER - DAY

Warm lights. A rented storefront dressed like care — soft chairs, lanyards, the same seal on a banner.

KEISHA (30s, two jobs, one kid) sits across from an OFFICIAL with a warm smile and a form.

OFFICIAL
Thank you for coming. We're here to
help.

Keisha nods, guarded.

OFFICIAL (CONT'D)
Just so we can respond quickly if
something happens.

The form wants everything — name, address, employer, school, phone, emergency contacts, social handles.

KEISHA
Why do you need all that.

OFFICIAL
So we can protect you.

KEISHA
What if I don't want to give it.

The official's smile tightens slightly.

OFFICIAL
Then we can't enroll you. We have to
verify eligibility.

Keisha looks around. Other people filling forms. No one looks happy. Just tired.

She signs.

The official hands her a grocery card and a pamphlet.

OFFICIAL (CONT'D)
Enrollment's done. We'll be in touch.

INT. GLASS OFFICE - DAY (NEXT DAY)

Keisha's SUPERVISOR at the desk. HR beside him, reading from a clipboard, not looking up.

HR
Unpaid leave. Pending review.

A printed sheet slides across the desk — a grainy photo of the circle, her face in the back row. Caption: EMPLOYEE ATTENDING EXTREMIST MISINFORMATION MEETINGS.

KEISHA
I enrolled in that program.

HR
The form listed us as your employer.

Keisha stares at the clipboard — her name, her address, her social handles, printed.

KEISHA
That's not help.

HR closes the clipboard.

INT. AUNTIE JO'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Soup simmers. Radio low. AUNTIE JO in the kitchen.

ONE at the table with a clean page and a pencil. SANDI across from him, hand on her stomach. HELEN beside, notebook open.

One writes, speaking each line as he tests it aloud.

ONE
FOURTH BRANCH ETHIC: CARE AND TRUTH.

He writes.

ONE (CONT'D)
One: Care without capture. If help
requires your identity, your loyalty,
or your silence — it's not help. It's
recruitment.

Helen writes without debate, copying.

ONE (CONT'D)
Two: Truth without spectacle. If truth
requires humiliation or rage or
performance, it's not truth. It's
theater.

Sandi listens, still.

ONE (CONT'D)
Three: Method over charisma. We do not
follow people. We follow tests.

Auntie Jo sets down bowls. No one stops writing.

ONE (CONT'D)
Four: Redundancy over centralization.
No single point of failure. No one
hero. No one archive. No one fund that
can be seized.

Five: Safety is not cowardice. If a
room is threatened, we reroute.

Six: Put it in writing. Power hates
paper trails. So we demand them.

Seven: Protect the vulnerable from
symbolization. Children are not
banners. Victims are not marketing.

He stops at seven. Sandi takes the pencil.

She writes in her own hand, slower than his:

SANDI
No names. No images. Not in any circle
material. Not the child. Not anyone's
child.

She underlines it. Hands the pencil back.

ONE
Eight: Never punish people for being
tired. The exhausted are the first
targets of capture. So we build
support. Not shame.

He sets the pencil down. The page sits between the three of them.

AUNTIE JO
Eat.

Helen reads the page once, top to bottom, then closes her notebook.

INT. AUNTIE JO'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Same table. Later. The ethic page dry beside the soup bowls.

ONE takes a second clean page. Writes a title, speaking it.

ONE
A Working Draft for Deliberation —
Fourth Branch.

He writes beneath it.

ONE (CONT'D)
Verification. Transparency. Public
Integrity. Human Oversight of
Machines.

Sandi watches. Helen copies.

ONE (CONT'D)
A public institution whose only job is
to protect conscience, transparency,
and long-term reality against
short-term domination.

He stops. Reads it back.

ONE (CONT'D)
It does not campaign. It does not
endorse candidates. It does not
produce propaganda. It does not answer
to donors.

SANDI
It does not exist to be popular.

One looks at her. Writes that down.

ONE
The point is to make it harder to lie,
steal, and disappear people.

He sets the pencil down. Folds the page once — with the ethic, with the checklist.

It stays on the table.

INT. CHURCH KITCHEN - DAY

DELPHINE at the stove. Soup on. A MAN in a city jacket at the door, clipboard.

INSPECTOR
City. Health and safety. Pending review.

He tapes a notice beside the door: NOTICE — PENDING REVIEW.

DELPHINE
This kitchen passed inspection for
twelve years.

INSPECTOR
Then it'll pass this one.

He works a padlock through the hasp.

INSPECTOR (CONT'D)
Until then, nobody in or out.

The padlock clicks. Delphine watches it. The soup keeps simmering behind her.

INT. ROW HOUSE - DAY

ROSA at the door. A CLERK with a single sheet.

CLERK
Housing authority. Your Community
Stability enrollment was never
completed.

ROSA
I never enrolled.

CLERK
That's the compliance problem.

He sets the sheet on the step. Doesn't wait for her to take it.

CLERK (CONT'D)
Missed enrollment. Review pending.

Rosa picks it up. Reads it once. The child watches from inside the doorway.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - DAY

HELEN at the reference desk. A MAN in a suit, polite.

OFFICIAL
The meeting room is under capacity
review. Effective immediately.

HELEN
We've used that room for years.

OFFICIAL
That's what's under review.

He leaves a single sheet on the desk. Helen doesn't open it.

INT. AUNTIE JO'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

The folded draft on the table.

DELPHINE (V.O.)
We have no kitchen.

HELEN (V.O.)
We have no room.

ONE sits at the table. He hears each one.

He does not open the draft.

INT. ROW HOUSE - NIGHT

ROSA sits on the front step. The notice in her hands.

Inside, through the window, the child watches.

She holds it.

FADE OUT.
